<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The growing diversity of large language models (LLMs) has motivated LLM routing as a paradigm for leveraging complementary capabilities, where different models are selected and coordinated according to their strengths, costs, and suitability for each query <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Depending on how model calls are organized, current routing approaches generally follow three paradigms: single-round routing selects a single model for one-shot execution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, multi-round routing makes successive model selections conditioned on intermediate outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and agentic routing organizes multiple models into structured collaborative workflows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>. Although routing decisions within each paradigm can adapt to the query, the execution paradigm itself is typically fixed in advance. As a result, the router can adapt <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which models</em> to use, but not <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how model calls should be organized</em> for different queries. This raises a natural question: <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how can we unify these routing paradigms within a single policy that jointly adapts execution mode and model selection to each query?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Unifying these routing paradigms within a single policy is non-trivial. Single-round, multi-round, and agentic routing follow different execution structures and decision processes, requiring a common formulation that accommodates all three while preserving their distinct behaviors. Beyond this structural challenge, two learning problems are particularly important. <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">First, different queries may favor different execution modes, yet determining which mode is appropriate requires reasoning about the task before its execution unfolds.</em> A unified router must therefore learn when single-round, multi-round, or agentic execution is most suitable for each query. <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Second, selecting an appropriate mode alone is insufficient, since each mode demands a different form of model selection and coordination.</em> Single-round routing requires choosing an effective model directly, multi-round routing must adapt subsequent selections to intermediate outputs, and agentic routing additionally organizes models through roles and dependencies. The router must therefore learn not only <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which mode to use</em>, but also <em id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how to execute effectively within the selected mode</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span>, a unified routing policy that learns to select among single-round, multi-round, and agentic execution paradigms and perform model selection within the chosen paradigm. We first introduce a unified routing format that expresses all three paradigms through a common action grammar for mode selection and subsequent routing decisions. To jointly optimize these two levels of decisions, we develop COMET-GRPO (<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CO</span>ordinated <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>ode–<span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E</span>xecution <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>raining), which decouples the trajectory-level advantage into separate signals for mode selection and within-mode execution, enabling targeted credit assignment to each decision level. We further introduce progressive exploration, which gradually shifts training from externally assigned modes to autonomous selection, allowing the router to first learn how to execute within each mode before deciding when to use it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> on benchmarks spanning mathematics, coding, knowledge, logical reasoning, and factual recall. <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> achieves 61.4% average accuracy, an 11.03% relative improvement over the strongest baseline, and leads all baselines across the five domains. We further study performance–cost trade-offs and generalization on held-out benchmarks (AIME, GPQA-D, and BBH). With <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, Worker inference cost decreases by 23% with only a 1.0-point accuracy drop, while <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> achieves 53.7% average accuracy on held-out benchmarks, an 18.28% relative improvement over the strongest baseline. Ablations and routing analyses further validate COMET-GRPO and progressive exploration and reveal adaptive execution choices across tasks.
Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate unified LLM routing across single-round, multi-round, and agentic execution paradigms, enabling a single policy to adapt both the execution mode and model selection to each query rather than committing to a predefined routing schema.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span>, featuring COMET-GRPO, which decouples credit assignment for mode selection and within-mode execution, together with progressive exploration that gradually transitions the router from learning how to execute each mode to autonomously deciding when to use it for different queries.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments across mathematics, coding, knowledge, logical reasoning, and factual recall demonstrate the effectiveness of unified routing over existing routers and fixed-mode strategies. Further analyses examine performance–cost trade-offs, routing behavior, and the contributions of the proposed training components.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Execution Paradigms for LLM Routing</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing LLM routing methods can be categorized by how model calls are organized.
(1) <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-round routing</span> selects one model for each query, typically based on model capability, query difficulty, or performance–cost trade-offs. RouteLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al., 2025</a>)</cite> learns routing from preference data, while RouterDC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> and GraphRouter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> model query–model relations. ICL-Router <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> further improves routing through learned model representations.
(2) <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-round routing</span> allows routing decisions to evolve across calls using intermediate outputs. Router-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> formulates routing as a sequential decision process with repeated model selection and response aggregation, while R2-Reasoner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2026</a>)</cite> combines task decomposition with model allocation for intermediate reasoning.
(3) <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agentic routing</span> organizes multiple models into collaborative workflows. MasRouter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2025</a>)</cite> jointly considers collaboration structures, agent roles, and model choices, while GraphPlanner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2026b</a>)</cite> generates query-dependent workflows with model–role assignments and explicit interactions.
Despite adaptive routing within each paradigm, prior approaches fix the execution paradigm in advance. In contrast, <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> unifies single-round, multi-round, and agentic routing as selectable modes of one policy, jointly learning mode selection and within-mode execution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Reinforcement Learning for Agentic LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a central approach for improving the reasoning and interactive capabilities of LLMs.
GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> replaces critic-based advantage estimation with group-relative rewards and has inspired several variants.
Dr.GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> addresses optimization biases in GRPO, while DAPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> improves training stability through asymmetric clipping and dynamic sampling.
GDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026</a>)</cite> further decouples reward normalization for multi-reward optimization.
RL has also been extended to interactive agents. Search-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and ReTool <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2026a</a>)</cite> train LLMs to interleave reasoning with search or tool interactions, while GiGPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> introduces episode- and step-level relative advantages for finer credit assignment in multi-turn agents.
Building on these advances, COMET-GRPO adapts group-relative optimization to unified LLM routing, where mode selection and within-mode execution form two coupled decision levels and receive separate credit signals.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a query <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and a pool of Worker models
<math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}=\{w_{1},\ldots,w_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}=\{w_{1},\ldots,w_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, a router <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> determines which Workers to invoke and
how their calls are organized. We refer to the resulting pattern of Worker
invocation and information flow as the <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">execution structure</em>. We distinguish
three execution paradigms according to how routing decisions are structured
(Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.19705v3/figure1.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/137;" width="548" height="137" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Three execution paradigms.
<span id="S3.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top left, single-round:</span> the router selects one Worker for a single call.
<span id="S3.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top right, multi-round:</span> the router selects Workers sequentially using accumulated observations.
<span id="S3.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom, agentic:</span> the router selects Workers, assigns roles, and specifies dependencies between calls; the depicted workflow is illustrative, and its roles and dependencies vary across queries.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-round routing.</span>
The router selects one Worker for the query and calls it once:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{single}}(x),\qquad o=w(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>single</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{single}}(x),\qquad o=w(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> is the selected Worker and <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> is its output. The resulting
execution structure consists of a single Worker call: the routing decision precedes
execution, with no subsequent Worker selection based on the returned response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-round routing.</span>
The router makes successive calls using earlier feedback. Let
<math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the sub-query generated for the <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th Worker call and
<math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t-1}=\big((w_{1},s_{1},o_{1}),\ldots,(w_{t-1},s_{t-1},o_{t-1})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t-1}=\big((w_{1},s_{1},o_{1}),\ldots,(w_{t-1},s_{t-1},o_{t-1})\big)</annotation></semantics></math> the ordered
history of preceding interactions. Each routing decision specifies a Worker and its
sub-query:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{t},s_{t})=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{multi}}(x,h_{t-1}),\qquad o_{t}=w_{t}(s_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>multi</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{t},s_{t})=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{multi}}(x,h_{t-1}),\qquad o_{t}=w_{t}(s_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The response is appended to the history before the next decision. This sequential
execution structure allows intermediate outputs to guide model selection, task
refinement, and further reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agentic routing.</span>
Calls are organized into a workflow with explicit roles and dependencies. We
describe the completed workflow after <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> calls by <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}=(V_{t},E_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}=(V_{t},E_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
indexes completed Worker calls and an edge <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,k)\in E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,k)\in E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that call <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
uses the output of call <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. A routing decision is</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{t},s_{t},r_{t},P_{t})=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{agentic}}(x,G_{t-1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>agentic</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{t},s_{t},r_{t},P_{t})=\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{agentic}}(x,G_{t-1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the role, such as planning, execution, verification, or
summarization, and <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}\subseteq V_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}\subseteq V_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of completed predecessor calls
selected as dependencies. The outputs <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{j}:j\in P_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{j}:j\in P_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are supplied as context to
Worker <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which executes sub-query <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> under role <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and its output is
recorded in the workflow. By selecting <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the router determines the direct
dependencies of each call. The resulting dependency structure supports branching and
aggregation, while independent calls may execute in parallel. Agentic workflows can
also be sequential; their defining distinction here is the explicit organization of
roles and dependencies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the above formulation, <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> unifies single-round, multi-round, and agentic routing in one policy. This raises three challenges. First, their heterogeneous execution structures require a common representation. Second, performance depends jointly on mode selection and within-mode execution, calling for differentiated learning signals. Third, mode selection requires sufficient experience across modes to avoid early bias. <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> addresses these challenges through a unified routing formulation (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Unified Trajectory Representation and Execution ‣ 4 RouteWeaver ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), COMET-GRPO for coordinated mode–execution learning (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 COMET-GRPO ‣ 4 RouteWeaver ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), and progressive exploration toward autonomous routing (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Progressive Exploration ‣ 4 RouteWeaver ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.19705v3/figure2.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/167;" width="548" height="167" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of <span id="S4.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span>. <span id="S4.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> One routing policy <math id="S4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> selects an execution mode, then calls frozen Workers;Multi-round calls automatically use the latest observation, while agentic calls include role assignments and router-selected references to earlier outputs.Returned observations are appended to its context until it emits the final answer.
<span id="S4.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> COMET-GRPO computes mode-level and within-mode advantages per reward component, mixes them (Eq. (7)), and assigns <math id="S4.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mode</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}</annotation></semantics></math> to mode-selection tokens and <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>inner</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}</annotation></semantics></math> to subsequent router tokens.
<span id="S4.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c)</span> Progressive training moves mode selection from assigned modes to the router in three stages.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Unified Trajectory Representation and Execution</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we represent each routing process as a mode-conditioned trajectory.
Given a query <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the router first selects an execution mode</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x),\qquad m\in\mathcal{M}=\{\mathrm{single},\mathrm{multi},\mathrm{agentic}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>single</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>multi</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>agentic</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x),\qquad m\in\mathcal{M}=\{\mathrm{single},\mathrm{multi},\mathrm{agentic}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and subsequently generates routing actions conditioned on the query and the
accumulated interaction context. Each Worker call specifies a Worker <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and a
sub-query <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>; agentic calls additionally specify a role <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and references <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to
completed calls. Multi-round execution passes the previous observation to each
subsequent call, while agentic execution passes the referenced outputs to
role-structured and potentially parallel calls. Single-round execution contains
exactly one Worker call. The serialization format and mode-specific constraints are
detailed in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Action Format ‣ Appendix A Routing Interface ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Executing an action returns the Worker outputs as an observation <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is
appended to the router context before the next decision. The router continues issuing
mode-compatible actions until it produces the final answer <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(m,a_{1},o_{1},\ldots,a_{T},o_{T},y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(m,a_{1},o_{1},\ldots,a_{T},o_{T},y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The mode, routing actions, and final answer are generated by the router and optimized
as policy tokens, whereas Worker observations and other environment-inserted content
are used only as context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Each trajectory receives a task reward and an efficiency reward,</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{\mathrm{task}},r_{\mathrm{eff}})=\begin{cases}(1,\,S_{\mathrm{cost}}(\tau)),&amp;\tau\text{ is valid and correct},\\[2.0pt]
(0,\,0),&amp;\tau\text{ is valid but incorrect},\\[2.0pt]
(-1,\,-1),&amp;\tau\text{ is terminal-invalid},\end{cases}\qquad S_{\mathrm{cost}}(\tau)=\frac{1}{1+C(\tau)/C_{\mathrm{ref}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>cost</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is valid and correct</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is valid but incorrect</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is terminal-invalid</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>cost</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{\mathrm{task}},r_{\mathrm{eff}})=\begin{cases}(1,\,S_{\mathrm{cost}}(\tau)),&amp;\tau\text{ is valid and correct},\\[2.0pt]
(0,\,0),&amp;\tau\text{ is valid but incorrect},\\[2.0pt]
(-1,\,-1),&amp;\tau\text{ is terminal-invalid},\end{cases}\qquad S_{\mathrm{cost}}(\tau)=\frac{1}{1+C(\tau)/C_{\mathrm{ref}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Correctness is determined by the task-specific scorer (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Evaluation Protocol ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>),
while <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> measures the fixed-reference inference cost of Worker calls, excluding
router computation, and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{ref}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{ref}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a frozen reference cost
(Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Rewards and Cost Accounting ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>). Terminal-invalid trajectories arise from grammar or
execution-constraint violations. These trajectory-level signals jointly reflect mode
selection and subsequent execution, motivating the decision-specific credit
assignment introduced next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>COMET-GRPO</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Joint mode selection and execution create an ambiguous credit-assignment problem. A
low-reward trajectory may result from selecting an unsuitable execution mode, from
executing an otherwise suitable mode poorly, or from both. Standard
GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> does not
distinguish these cases: it assigns the same trajectory-level relative advantage to the
mode-selection tokens and all subsequent router-generated tokens. We therefore
decompose relative feedback into two complementary comparisons: an across-mode
comparison for mode selection and a within-mode comparison for execution conditioned
on the selected mode. COMET-GRPO (<em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">COordinated Mode–Execution Training</em>) uses
these two signals to optimize the two decision levels separately within a shared
policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Two levels of relative advantage.</span>
For each query, we sample a group of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts; all statistics below are computed
within this group and separately for each reward component. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the
rollouts retained for advantage estimation (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Rewards and Cost Accounting ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>). For one
component with values <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be the mean and standard deviation
of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r_{i}\}_{i\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{i}\}_{i\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that GRPO assigns
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{\mathrm{GRPO}}=(r_{i}-\mu)/(\sigma+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>GRPO</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{\mathrm{GRPO}}=(r_{i}-\mu)/(\sigma+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> to every router token of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In a <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">free</em> group, each rollout selects its own mode, so the group need not
cover all modes. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{m}\subseteq\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{m}\subseteq\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> contain the retained
rollouts that selected <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, with mean <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of the same component. For rollout <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
with mode <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}=\frac{\mu_{m_{i}}-\mu}{\sigma+\epsilon},\qquad A_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}=\frac{r_{i}-\mu_{m_{i}}}{\sigma+\epsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mode</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>inner</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}=\frac{\mu_{m_{i}}-\mu}{\sigma+\epsilon},\qquad A_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}=\frac{r_{i}-\mu_{m_{i}}}{\sigma+\epsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mode</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}</annotation></semantics></math> measures how the average reward of the selected mode
compares with the overall rollout-group average for the same query, whereas
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>inner</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}</annotation></semantics></math> measures how this particular trajectory compares with the
average trajectory under the same mode. Both use the group standard deviation <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> rather than a within-mode
one, keeping the two signals on a common scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following the reward-wise normalization of GDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we compute these
advantages separately for the task and efficiency rewards and then mix them:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{l}=(1-\alpha)\,A_{i,\mathrm{task}}^{l}+\alpha\,A_{i,\mathrm{eff}}^{l},\qquad l\in\{\mathrm{mode},\mathrm{inner}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>task</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>eff</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>mode</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>inner</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{l}=(1-\alpha)\,A_{i,\mathrm{task}}^{l}+\alpha\,A_{i,\mathrm{eff}}^{l},\qquad l\in\{\mathrm{mode},\mathrm{inner}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">With <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> only the task signal is used; <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> adds the efficiency
signal. The weight <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed within a run and is independent of the
exploration schedule of Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Progressive Exploration ‣ 4 RouteWeaver ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, and no further batch-level
normalization is applied. Component-wise definitions are given in
Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Optimization Details ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decision-specific credit assignment.</span>
Having separated the two sources of feedback, we apply each signal only to the
decisions it is intended to supervise. The mixed <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mode</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned to the tokens of the mode decision,
<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mode</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>inner</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the subsequent router tokens,
<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>inner</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}</annotation></semantics></math>: Worker choices, roles, references, sub-queries, and the final
answer. Observations, injected content, and padding are masked. Following the PPO
clipped surrogate objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, for a router token
<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math> with context <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math> (indexed by <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to distinguish tokens from interaction
steps), the clipped token loss is</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{ik}(A)=-\min\!\big[r_{ik}A,\;\operatorname{clip}(r_{ik},1-\eta_{-},1+\eta_{+})A\big],\qquad r_{ik}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(z_{ik}\mid H_{ik})}{\pi_{\mathrm{old}}(z_{ik}\mid H_{ik})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>old</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{ik}(A)=-\min\!\big[r_{ik}A,\;\operatorname{clip}(r_{ik},1-\eta_{-},1+\eta_{+})A\big],\qquad r_{ik}=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(z_{ik}\mid H_{ik})}{\pi_{\mathrm{old}}(z_{ik}\mid H_{ik})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">with dual clipping for negative advantages as specified in
Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Optimization Details ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Joint policy optimization.</span>
Each of the two losses is normalized by its own global count of effective tokens in the
mini-batch, and the results are added:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}=\frac{\sum_{i}\sum_{k\in S_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}}\ell_{ik}(A_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}})}{N_{\mathrm{mode}}}+\frac{\sum_{i}\sum_{k\in S_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}}\ell_{ik}(A_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}})}{N_{\mathrm{inner}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>PG</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mode</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>mode</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mode</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>inner</mi></msubsup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>inner</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>inner</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}=\frac{\sum_{i}\sum_{k\in S_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}}}\ell_{ik}(A_{i}^{\mathrm{mode}})}{N_{\mathrm{mode}}}+\frac{\sum_{i}\sum_{k\in S_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}}}\ell_{ik}(A_{i}^{\mathrm{inner}})}{N_{\mathrm{inner}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{mode}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>mode</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{mode}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{inner}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>inner</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{inner}}</annotation></semantics></math> count the effective mode and inner
tokens in the mini-batch, and an empty term is skipped. Mode tokens are typically far
fewer than execution tokens, so averaging over all tokens together would give the
mode term a small aggregate weight merely because it is short; separate normalization
removes this dependence on length. The policy is regularized toward the frozen initial router
<math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, distinct from <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{old}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>old</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{old}}</annotation></semantics></math>, using a KL penalty
with coefficient <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}=\frac{1}{N_{\mathrm{KL}}}\sum_{(i,k)\in\Omega_{\mathrm{KL}}}\kappa_{ik}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}=\frac{1}{N_{\mathrm{KL}}}\sum_{(i,k)\in\Omega_{\mathrm{KL}}}\kappa_{ik},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the full effective policy mask, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> its
token count, and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{ik}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{ik}</annotation></semantics></math> a per-token estimate of the KL divergence to
<math id="S4.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The estimator,
masks, and degenerate groups are described in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Optimization Details ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Progressive Exploration</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the router chose modes autonomously from the start, early preferences could reduce
the training opportunities of the other modes. Progressive exploration therefore
first provides execution experience within each mode and then gradually hands mode
selection to the router, in three phases indexed by the data-batch index <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. Each query is <em id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">forced</em> with
probability</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{b}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;b&lt;b_{0},\\[2.0pt]
1-\dfrac{b-b_{0}}{b_{1}-b_{0}},&amp;b_{0}\leq b&lt;b_{1},\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;b\geq b_{1},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{b}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;b&lt;b_{0},\\[2.0pt]
1-\dfrac{b-b_{0}}{b_{1}-b_{0}},&amp;b_{0}\leq b&lt;b_{1},\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;b\geq b_{1},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and otherwise <em id="S4.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">free</em>; all <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts of a query share this condition. A forced
query receives one externally assigned mode, with modes distributed across the batch,
and its injected mode prefix is masked.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cold start (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;b_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;b_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
This phase teaches execution within each mode before the router selects modes. All
queries are forced. We denote by <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}^{\mathrm{global}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>GRPO</mi><mi>global</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}^{\mathrm{global}}</annotation></semantics></math> the
ordinary GRPO loss, obtained by averaging <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{ik}(A_{i}^{\mathrm{GRPO}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>GRPO</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{ik}(A_{i}^{\mathrm{GRPO}})</annotation></semantics></math> over all
effective router tokens (Eq. <a href="#A3.E17" title="In Ordinary GRPO loss. ‣ C.2 Optimization Details ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>). The router is updated with
<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}^{\mathrm{global}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>GRPO</mi><mi>global</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}^{\mathrm{global}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the task reward alone; advantage
decomposition and the support term are inactive. All runs start independently from
the same initial router at step 0; after this phase each run continues its own
training trajectory with its fixed <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Hyperparameters and Decoding ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Forced/free transition (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{0}\leq b&lt;b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{0}\leq b&lt;b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
Forced groups decrease and free groups increase. Free groups use the advantages of
Eq. <a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.2 COMET-GRPO ‣ 4 RouteWeaver ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Forced groups compute <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{\mathrm{GRPO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>GRPO</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{\mathrm{GRPO}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each enabled
reward component, mix them with the same weights, and apply the result to their
execution tokens, which enter the inner term of Eq. <a href="#S4.E9" title="In 4.2 COMET-GRPO ‣ 4 RouteWeaver ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>; their injected
mode tokens remain masked (Eq. <a href="#A3.E18" title="In Mixed-batch token advantages. ‣ C.2 Optimization Details ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>). To keep probability support on
every mode and prevent it from concentrating prematurely, we add a one-sided penalty
on <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{p}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{p}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the normalized score of candidate mode <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> averaged over the unique
queries in the batch (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Optimization Details ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{support}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}|}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\max\!\left(0,\log\delta-\log(\bar{p}_{m}+\epsilon)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>support</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.808em;" width="1.808em"><mi>max</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{support}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}|}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\max\!\left(0,\log\delta-\log(\bar{p}_{m}+\epsilon)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Because it acts on batch-level averages, individual queries can still favor different
modes. Its coefficient <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{b}=\lambda_{0}\rho_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{b}=\lambda_{0}\rho_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> applies only during the
transition; the term is inactive in the other phases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fully autonomous routing (<math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
All queries are free, and the router jointly learns mode selection and execution with
COMET-GRPO.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phase objectives.</span>
Across the three phases, the router minimizes</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{b}=\begin{cases}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}^{\mathrm{global}}+\beta\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}},&amp;b&lt;b_{0},\\[2.0pt]
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}+\beta\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}+\lambda_{b}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{support}},&amp;b_{0}\leq b&lt;b_{1},\\[2.0pt]
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}+\beta\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}},&amp;b\geq b_{1},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>GRPO</mi><mi>global</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>PG</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>support</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>PG</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{b}=\begin{cases}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}^{\mathrm{global}}+\beta\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}},&amp;b&lt;b_{0},\\[2.0pt]
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}+\beta\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}}+\lambda_{b}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{support}},&amp;b_{0}\leq b&lt;b_{1},\\[2.0pt]
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}+\beta\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{KL}},&amp;b\geq b_{1},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}^{\mathrm{global}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>GRPO</mi><mi>global</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{GRPO}}^{\mathrm{global}}</annotation></semantics></math> uses the task-only GRPO advantage
and <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>PG</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PG}}</annotation></semantics></math> uses the <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-mixed advantages, including the mixed
GRPO advantages of forced groups in its inner term. Phase boundaries and coefficients
are reported in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Hyperparameters and Decoding ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
