<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> across five task domains to examine whether jointly selecting and
executing different routing modes improves over methods restricted to a fixed
execution paradigm. We further study its performance–cost trade-off, isolate the
contributions of COMET-GRPO and progressive exploration, and analyze how
execution-mode choices vary across tasks. Additional dataset, evaluation, and
implementation details are provided in the appendices.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
Our evaluation comprises nine benchmarks spanning five domains: <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Math</span>,
MATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021b</a>)</cite> and Omni-MATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code</span>,
APPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>, LiveCodeBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and
TACO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Knowledge</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text">SuperGPQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2025</a>)</cite>;
<span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reason</span>, SATBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; and <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recall</span>,
TriviaQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joshi et al., 2017</a>)</cite> and
SimpleQA-Verified <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haas et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Test questions do not overlap with
the training questions used for router optimization. Dataset composition, training
pools, and evaluation sets are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Datasets ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Router and Worker models.</span>
The router is Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> with all parameters
fine-tuned. The Worker pool
contains six frozen models: Qwen3-8B and
Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and
Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Llama Team, AI @ Meta, 2024</a>)</cite>,
Gemma-3-27B-IT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemma Team, 2025</a>)</cite>, and
Gemini-2.5-Flash-Lite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemini Team, Google, 2025</a>)</cite>. The router refers to Workers only
by anonymous IDs with capability descriptions and never sees model names or prices.
Model identifiers and serving providers are listed in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Router and Worker Models ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We compare <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> with ten routers spanning all three execution paradigms.
Single-round baselines include Router-KNN and Router-MLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>,
RouterDC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, and GraphRouter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>;
multi-round baselines include Prompt LLM and Router-KNN-MR following
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, R2-Reasoner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and
Router-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>;
and agentic baselines include MasRouter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and
GraphPlanner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
