<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">RouteWeaver<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> substantially outperforms fixed-paradigm routers.</span></span>
Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2 Performance Across Execution Paradigms ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> compares <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> with single-round, multi-round, and agentic routing
baselines across the five evaluation domains. <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> reaches an average accuracy of
61.4%, an 11.03% relative improvement over the strongest baseline (55.3%,
Router-R1). Its relative improvements over the strongest single-round, multi-round, and
agentic baselines are 26.08%, 11.03%, and 31.20%, respectively, and it leads all
baselines in each of the five domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive execution provides broad but task-dependent gains.</span>
<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> leads the strongest baseline in every evaluation domain, with relative
improvements ranging from 5.20% on Recall to 12.52% on Math. Importantly, the strongest
fixed-paradigm baseline varies across domains: multi-round routers perform best among
the baselines on Math, Code, and Knowledge, whereas single-round routers are strongest
on Reason and Recall. This variation suggests that no single execution
paradigm is uniformly preferable across tasks, motivating <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span>’s query-adaptive mode
selection. Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Routing Behavior and Generalization ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> further examines how <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> distributes
queries across execution modes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Performance–Cost Trade-off</h3>

<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.19705v3/figure3.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:274/225;" width="274" height="225"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Fixed-reference Worker inference cost per query against average accuracy. The
curve shows <span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> trained with <math id="S5.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{0,0.1,0.3,0.5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{0,0.1,0.3,0.5\}</annotation></semantics></math>; markers show the two
strongest baselines of each execution paradigm in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2 Performance Across Execution Paradigms ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The <math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> cost
is reconstructed from token usage.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency preferences allow <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> to operate at different accuracy–cost
points.</span>
The main results use <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> and optimize task performance only, whereas different
deployments may require different balances between accuracy and cost. We therefore
evaluate <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> with <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{0,0.1,0.3,0.5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{0,0.1,0.3,0.5\}</annotation></semantics></math>, keeping all other settings
fixed; Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.3 Performance–Cost Trade-off ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the resulting operating points. As <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>
increases, Worker cost per query decreases monotonically, accompanied by a gradual
decrease in accuracy.
Relative to <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> lowers cost by 23% while losing 1.0 point of
accuracy, and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.3</annotation></semantics></math> lowers it by 61% while losing 3.1 points; at
<math id="S5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, cost falls by 86% while accuracy drops by 11.2 points. These results
illustrate different operating points that trade accuracy for efficiency under
different deployment preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost-aware routing provides different operating points under the same unified
policy.</span>
All operating points are obtained from the same unified routing framework by changing
only <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Routing Behavior and Generalization ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> analyzes how <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> distributes
execution modes across domains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Ablation of COMET-GRPO and Progressive Exploration</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.4 Ablation of COMET-GRPO and Progressive Exploration ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> ablates the two learning components of <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span>. Both variants
share the router initialization, Worker pool, training data, evaluation protocol, and
training budget of the full model, and all use <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w/o COMET-GRPO.</span>
Replacing COMET-GRPO with ordinary GRPO reduces average accuracy from 61.4% to
58.8% and shifts mode usage from .35/.41/.24 to .13/.82/.05, strongly concentrating
the policy on multi-round execution. The largest drop occurs on Knowledge (5.8
points). This suggests that decision-specific credit assignment helps preserve
differentiated mode selection rather than letting one execution mode dominate.</p>
</div>
