<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Routing Behavior and Generalization</h3>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.19705v3/figure4.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/185;" width="274" height="185"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Share of selected execution modes across evaluation domains for <span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> (<math id="S5.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>). Each bar shows the proportion of test queries routed to single-round,
multi-round, and agentic execution.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Routing behavior across domains.</span>
Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.5 Routing Behavior and Generalization ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reveals strongly task-dependent execution preferences. Math
uses all three modes, with 19% of queries routed to single-round, 50% to multi-round,
and 31% to agentic execution. Code is dominated by multi-round routing (84%), whereas
Knowledge primarily uses single-round execution (72%). The contrast is strongest for
Reason and Recall: 99% of reasoning queries are routed to agentic execution, while
96% of recall queries use single-round routing. These results reveal substantially
different execution-mode preferences across task domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalization to held-out benchmarks.</span>
To evaluate whether the learned routing policy transfers beyond the datasets used for
router training, we further test <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> on AIME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI-MO, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Math-AI Team, 2025</a>)</cite>
for Math, GPQA-Diamond (GPQA-D) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rein et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for Knowledge, and
BBH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suzgun et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for Reason. We compare against one
representative baseline from each execution paradigm (Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.5 Routing Behavior and Generalization ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
<span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> achieves the best accuracy on all three held-out benchmarks, reaching an average
of 53.7%, an 18.28% relative improvement over Router-R1 (45.4%). The gains are
consistent across all three benchmarks, with improvements of 11.4, 7.1, and 6.4 points over Router-R1 on AIME, GPQA-D, and BBH, respectively. The learned execution behavior also varies across held-out benchmarks,
with agentic execution accounting for 75% of AIME queries but only 12% of BBH
queries. These results indicate that <span id="S5.SS5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span>’s adaptive routing behavior extends to
unseen benchmarks within the same task domains.</p>
</div>
