<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span>, a unified LLM routing framework that treats the execution paradigm
itself as a routing decision, jointly learning mode selection and within-mode
execution. By combining COMET-GRPO with progressive exploration, <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> learns when to
use single-round, multi-round, or agentic execution and how to route effectively
within the selected mode. Across five task domains, <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> consistently outperforms
fixed-paradigm routing baselines, exhibits task-dependent execution preferences, and
supports different performance–cost operating points. These results suggest that
effective LLM routing should adapt not only which models to invoke, but also how their
computation is organized. Extending this unified perspective to richer execution
structures and tool-augmented environments is an interesting direction for future
work.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">AI use statement</h3>

<div id="S6.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we used generative AI tools to assist with implementation and debugging,
drafting and editing portions of the manuscript for clarity, and surveying and
summarizing relevant literature. All AI-assisted code and manuscript content were
reviewed by the authors, and AI-assisted code was inspected and tested before use. The
authors take full responsibility for the final content, claims, and artifacts
presented in this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Ethics statement</h3>

<div id="S6.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work studies routing among existing language models using established benchmarks
and does not involve human-subject experiments or the collection of private user data.
<span id="S6.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> inherits the limitations and potential harms of its underlying Worker models,
including biased, unsafe, or factually incorrect outputs, which may propagate across
multiple model calls. Our experiments are intended to evaluate routing methodology
rather than safety-critical deployment. Practical deployments should therefore apply
appropriate model-level safety mechanisms and comply with the licenses and usage
policies of the underlying models and datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Reproducibility statement</h3>

<div id="S6.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide the complete formulation and training objectives of <span id="S6.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span> in
Section <a href="#S4" title="4 RouteWeaver ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, and describe the datasets, router and Worker models, and
baselines in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. The appendices further document the routing
serialization and execution constraints (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Action Format ‣ Appendix A Routing Interface ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>), dataset
construction and evaluation sets (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Datasets ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>), task-specific evaluation
procedures (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Evaluation Protocol ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>), model and serving configurations
(Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Router and Worker Models ‣ Appendix B Experimental Setup ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>), reward and cost definitions
(Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Rewards and Cost Accounting ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>), optimization details (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Optimization Details ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>),
and training hyperparameters, decoding settings, and
call budgets (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Hyperparameters and Decoding ‣ Appendix C Training Details ‣ RouteWeaver: Weaving Mode Selection and Execution into Unified LLM Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>). Together, these sections document the training
and evaluation setup used for <span id="S6.SSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RouteWeaver</span>. Code is available at
<a href="https://github.com/LaughKing/RouteWeaver" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/LaughKing/RouteWeaver</a>.</p>
</div>
